course: medicine
lesson: 49
title: Blisters, astro burns and nosebleeds mid-match
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A blister is your skin's own bubble wrap: when a stiff new boot rubs, the skin layers slide apart and fluid fills the gap to cushion the sore spot underneath.
explain: Harry in Sheffield wears brand-new boots straight into a match, «These are lush» (lush means lovely), and by half-time he's got a blister the size of a coin; breaking boots in slowly would have saved him.
explain: An astro burn is a friction burn: sliding on a 3G pitch, the artificial grass with tiny black rubber bits, scrapes off the top of the skin and can leave grit stuck in it.
explain: A nosebleed on the pitch means off until it stops: lean forward, pinch the soft part of the nose, and only come back once it has stopped and the blood is cleaned off your kit.
fact: Rubbing makes skin layers slide apart and fluid fills the gap, which is a blister; new stiff boots and wet socks rub most.
fact: Don't pop a blister, because the skin on top keeps germs out; cover it with a plaster or blister dressing.
fact: If a blister bursts, leave the loose skin in place and cover it, rather than peeling it off.
fact: A blister that becomes red, hot, swollen or full of yellow pus may be infected and needs a pharmacist or GP.
fact: Breaking new boots in a little at a time, and changing wet socks at half-time, makes blisters less likely.
fact: An astro burn is a friction burn that scrapes the top layers of skin off and can trap grit or rubber crumbs.
fact: Rinse an astro burn well under cool clean running water for about 10 minutes, cover it with a non-stick dressing, and tell an adult.
fact: Toothpaste, butter or creams on a fresh friction burn can trap heat and germs; cool clean water is what helps.
fact: Artificial grass scrapes bare skin more than real grass, so long socks, leggings or sliding shorts protect the legs.
fact: In many sports a bleeding player must leave the pitch until the bleeding stops and blood on the kit is cleaned or the kit changed.
fact: For a nosebleed: sit, lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose for 10 to 15 minutes; tipping the head back sends blood down the throat, which can make you sick.
fact: A nosebleed that hasn't stopped after 10 to 15 minutes of pinching means an adult takes you to get checked.
fact: A nose that looks crooked after a hit may be broken, and a blow to the face can also cause concussion, so the player is off for the day and an adult gets it checked.
fact: «Minging» is British slang for disgusting.
exercise: What is the fluid in a blister for? | It cushions the sore skin underneath | It is pus from an infection under the skin | It is sweat stuck under the skin | It is blood that has turned clear
exercise: What causes most sport blisters? | Rubbing, from new boots or wet socks | Eating too many sweets | Cold weather on the feet | Running on real grass
exercise: Should you pop a blister? | No, the skin on top keeps germs out | Yes, with a clean pin, so it dries out | Yes, popping makes it heal faster | Only if it's bigger than a coin
exercise: Your blister has burst. What should you do with the loose skin? | Leave it and cover it with a plaster | Peel it all off so the skin underneath can dry | Cut it off with scissors | Rub it with mud to toughen it
exercise: Which is a sign that a blister is infected? | It is red, hot and has yellow pus | It is a small bubble full of clear fluid | It is flat, dry and doesn't hurt at all | It went away after two days
exercise: Who should see an infected blister? | A pharmacist or GP | Nobody, it heals itself | The PE teacher's assistant | The referee at the next match
exercise: How can you avoid blisters from new boots? | Break them in a little at a time | Wear them for a whole tournament first | Wear no socks with them | Soak them in water overnight
exercise: What is an astro burn? | A friction burn from sliding on artificial grass | A burn from a hot pitch heater | A sunburn from playing in summer | A rash from grass pollen
exercise: First aid for an astro burn? | Rinse in cool clean water and cover it | Rub toothpaste on it to cool the sting | Leave it dirty so it can scab over by itself | Put butter on it to cool it
exercise: Why not put toothpaste on a friction burn? | It can trap heat and germs | It makes the skin minty fresh | It is far too expensive to use on skin | It stains your shorts and socks white
exercise: How can players protect their legs on astro? | Long socks, leggings or sliding shorts | Rubbing sun cream on the knees before they play | Playing in bare feet | Sliding faster so it hurts less
exercise: A player's nose is bleeding in the match. What does the ref usually do? | Sends them off until the bleeding stops | Lets them play on if they wipe it on their shirt | Gives them a yellow card for bleeding | Stops the match for the day
exercise: What's the right way to stop a nosebleed? | Lean forward and pinch the soft part | Tip your head right back to stop the flow | Lie flat on the pitch | Blow your nose very hard
exercise: How long should you pinch? | About 10 to 15 minutes | About 10 seconds, then let go | Until the ref blows the whistle again | Only one quick, very hard squeeze
exercise: A nose looks crooked after an elbow. What happens? | Off for the day and an adult gets it checked | Straight back on after a wipe | Push it straight on the bench | Play on and see at full time
test: Oisín in Galway: «Sure I'll peel the skin off so it dries out». Good idea? | No, leave the skin on and cover it | Yes, peeled blisters heal fastest in the air | Yes, and add some salt to dry it out | No, he should pop the other ones as well
test: Leah's blister, 3 days on: red, hot, yellow gunk. «It's minging». What next? | Tell an adult and see a pharmacist or GP | Pop it again with a pin to let all the gunk out | Ignore it, all blisters heal | Cover it with tape and run
test: Amara at half-time, socks soaked, «my heel's raw, it's giving pain». Best move? | A plaster on the spot and dry socks | Take her boots off for the second half | Play on, blisters are for wimps | Pour more water in to cool it
test: Ayesha slides on 3G, black rubber bits in her thigh: «Bloody hell, that stings!» What first? | Rinse it well in cool clean water | Pick the bits out with her dirty fingers | Rub some mud in to stop the sting | Cover it at once, bits and all
test: Ruben's mate: «Toothpaste on it, it's cooling». What's actually right? | Cool clean water, then a clean dressing | Toothpaste, then a clean sock over it | Butter, then a bandage | Ice cream straight on the sore skin
test: Ella: «astro's soft, so sliding's safe, innit». Right? | No, it scrapes bare skin badly | Yes, it's much softer than real grass | Yes, as long as you slide fast enough | No, because astro gets hot in the sun
test: Finn: «I'll wipe it, I'm grand». The ref sends him off with his nosebleed. Fair? | Yes, bleeding players go off until it stops | No, wiping it is enough | No, nosebleeds don't count | Yes, and he's banned for the season
test: A dad shouts «Head back, lad!» at Josh's nosebleed. What would happen if he did? | Blood would run down his throat and could make him sick | The bleeding would stop at once | His nose would cool down faster | Nothing different would happen
test: Harry wants to wear new boots straight into the cup final. Why not? | Stiff new boots rub and cause blisters | New boots are against the rules in a cup final | They make you slower for a year | Boots need a whole season to dry
test: Maya's nose is bleeding and crooked after an elbow at basketball. «Put me back in». Why not? | It may be broken and she may be concussed | The ref doesn't like seeing blood on the court | Crooked noses make you miss shots | Only the coach can bleed on court
test: A nosebleed is still going after 15 minutes of pinching. What now? | An adult takes her to get it checked | Pinch even harder for another whole hour | Tip her head back to change things | Play on, it'll stop eventually
exam: Identify the principal function of the fluid contained within a blister. | It cushions the damaged skin beneath | It constitutes evidence of infection beneath the skin | It is accumulated perspiration | It is blood deprived of colour
exam: Select the response consistent with current advice concerning the management of a blister. | The blister is to be left intact and covered | The blister is to be punctured with a needle and drained | The overlying skin is to be removed | The blister is to be exposed to the air uncovered
exam: It is given that a participant has sustained a friction burn upon an artificial surface. State the appropriate initial treatment. | Irrigation with cool, clean running water | Application of toothpaste | Application of butter | Covering without prior cleansing
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the treatment of a nosebleed? | Leaning forward and compressing the soft part of the nose | Tilting the head backwards | Lying supine upon the ground | Blowing the nose forcefully
